{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1576873633,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI5MDE5NTc5OTM="},"added":["UX"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1576911773,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2ODE1ODc2NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3953#issuecomment-568158764"},"message":"FTR: I noticed yesterday a related pattern where results are not re-emitted by intermediate code in order to prevent flooding the output with pointless messages. This is (now if not always) a problem, because such action cannot be acted upon anymore (think result hooks). Decisions in UI behavior and logs should be made by UI code and not by the respective result handling code.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1577023261,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2ODIwODI4Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3953#issuecomment-568208286"},"message":"\u003e With the introduction of log.result-level, we should revisit these custom_result_renderer checks.\n\nI am uncertain what action is best. If we are OK to start treating *any* logging as optional and not part of the main/mandatory communication with a user, things are clear and we unconditionally operate on any result, regardless of configuration. If we cannot agree on that, we would have to query the configuration for each result and infer what would be best.\n\nIMHO the former is the only sensible way to go. Otherwise we would say that critical information *on a result extracted from a particular result record* does not make it to a result renderer (i.e. does not make it outside the command, hence cannot be acted upon in any way).\n\nA complication comes from the combination of result renderer selection and `result_xfm` settings. It looks as if presently setting a `result_xfm` (which is done by default in cases like `install`) turns of any execution of \"tailored\" result renderers (and possibly also renders hooks unusable, because `result_xfm` is applied before any evaluation of original result records is done).","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1577023261,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMyODY4NzczNA=="},"target":"449a71c4effc73d691a4773c64bc5b576658d076633ae06c88f3e48584938359","message":"\u003e With the introduction of log.result-level, we should revisit these custom_result_renderer checks.\n\nI am uncertain what action is best. If we are OK to start treating *any* logging as optional and not part of the main/mandatory communication with a user, things are clear and we unconditionally operate on any result, regardless of configuration. If we cannot agree on that, we would have to query the configuration for each result and infer what would be best.\n\nIMHO the former is the only sensible way to go. Otherwise we would say that critical information *on a result extracted from a particular result record* does not make it to a result renderer (i.e. does not make it outside the command, hence cannot be acted upon in any way).\n\nA complication comes from the combination of result renderer selection and `result_xfm` settings. It looks as if presently setting a `result_xfm` (which is done by default in cases like `install`) turns off any execution of \"tailored\" result renderers (and possibly also renders hooks unusable, because `result_xfm` is applied before any evaluation of original result records is done).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1577111375,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2ODQ5MDI0Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3953#issuecomment-568490247"},"message":"\u003e If we are OK to start treating _any_ logging as optional and not part of the main/mandatory communication with a user,\n\nYes, that matches my view of logging, though my impression is that this is far from datalad's approach.\n\n\u003e things are clear and we unconditionally operate on any result, regardless of configuration.\n\u003e If we cannot agree on that, we would have to query the configuration for each result and infer what would be best.\n\nMy opinion is that we should unconditionally operate on any result.  That might lead to some double reporting, but `log.result-level` allows those to be demoted.\n\n\u003e IMHO the former is the only sensible way to go. Otherwise we would say that critical information _on a result extracted from a particular result record_ does not make it to a result renderer (i.e. does not make it outside the command, hence cannot be acted upon in any way).\n\nI agree.","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1578827979,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI5NDExNzEyMjI="},"added":["severity-important"],"removed":[]}]}